and that's going to do it for this podcast.

39
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
So let's get started, shall we?

40
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less?

41
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Not too many boys.

42
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
If you ask them, go ahead and ask them. ask your scouts.

43
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,000
Not too many scouts would say that

44
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
one of the big reasons that they joined scouting

45
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:55,000
was to attend troop meetings.

46
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
No, they want adventure, right.

47
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
They want to have fun with their friends.

48
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
They want to have fun,

49
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,000
They want to learn new skills,

50
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
They want to explore the outdoors.

51
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
But we put an awful lot of effort

52
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
and an awful lot of time into troop meetings

53
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
And troop meetings could be the catalyst

54
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
for all these great things that boys want to do as scouts.

55
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
They have to be fast-paced and interesting

56
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
and varied and engaging right,

57
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
And they need to lead towards those

58
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
exciting troop activities in the outdoors.

59
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
They need to be the kind of glue

60
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
that holds a troop together.

61
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:34,000
If we look at a troop meeting

62
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
as a couple of hours to fill once a week,

63
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
we're going to scramble for all kinds of different resources

64
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
and just try and program two hours of stuff.

65
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,000
I'm going to suggest that that's not a very good approach.

66
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Let's look at-

67
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Let's assume- you go camping once a month.

68
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
That means you have two or three troop meetings

69
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
to prepare for a camping trip

70
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
And let's say you meet for an hour and a half,

71
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
two hours every week.

72
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
So you've got somewhere between

73
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
four and six hours of trip preparation time

74
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:09,000
And that's what those troop meetings can be.

75
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
You've also got other purposes that you can serve.

76
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
It motivates.

77
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Are your troop meetings motivating?

78
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Do they motivate scouts?

79
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Do they motivate them to be better scouts

80
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:22,000
and to encourage them to learn

81
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:24,000
and practice scouting skills

82
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
and to advance in rank?

83
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Do the meetings strengthen patrols?

84
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Do they promote patrol spirit?

85
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And, more importantly,

86
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
are your troop meetings a laboratory of leadership?

87
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Are they allowing scouts to exercise leadership?

88
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
And troop meetings are especially effective at this

89
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
because they need to be planned,

90
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
organized and carried out by the boys themselves.

91
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Scouts take ownership in the meetings

92
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,000
and they're empowered to lead the events

93
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,000
that make up each meeting.

94
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Boys need the resources

95
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
to make their troop meetings successful

96
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:57,000
And as adult leaders

97
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
we can provide support and guidance

98
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
that will allow them to make the most of those resources.

99
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
But perhaps the most important resource

100
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
for them in developing effective troop meetings

101
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
is the troop meeting plan.

102
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Is there some kind of framework?

103
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
I'm going to talk about the one that

104
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
we all hear about all the time.

105
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
It has seven distinct steps.

106
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It's a format that's been developed

107
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
over many years of scouting. experience

108
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Presents scouts with an effective framework

109
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
to put together troop meetings

110
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
And so if they work within this framework,

111
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
it will give them some focus.

112
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
It will give them a framework

113
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:34,000
for enjoyable, productive,

114
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
meaningful and timely troop meetings.

115
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
So there's seven distinct steps.

116
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
There's the pre-opening part of the meeting.

117
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
There's the opening part of the meeting.

118
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
There's usually some kind of presentation

119
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
or skills instruction.

120
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:49,000
There's a time for patrols to meet.

121
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
There's an inter-patrol activity.

122
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
There's a closing

123
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
And then there's that time after the meeting.

124
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Let's say your troop meeting is 7 o'clock.

125
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
Your adult and youth leaders

126
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
begin to arrive around 6.30

127
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
in preparation for the meeting.

128
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Now we take advantage of that time

129
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
in my troop to have the patrol leaders

130
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
council get together

131
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and go over whatever plans

132
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
they have for that meeting

133
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
And just to kind of get things

134
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:17,000
set up and staged and ready to go.

135
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,000
But there's also this pre-opening,

136
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
because as boys begin to arrive

137
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
for the meeting, as scouts begin to arrive

138
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
for the meeting, there should be

139
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
something going on-

140
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Any kind of a game or project

141
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
or something like that-

142
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
designed so that the scouts

143
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
can join in as they show up.

144
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And that person who is in charge of that

145
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:39,000
should be ready to start about 15 minutes early,

146
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
Right,

147
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
And keep that activity going all the way up

148
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
until it's time to begin.

149
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
And when we begin, we're at the opening,

150
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
And when we begin the meeting,

151
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
that's the opening of the meeting.

152
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
That is, the senior patrol leader

153
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:55,000
is going to run that,

154
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
He's going to call the meeting to order,

155
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
He's going to have the scouts

156
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
line up or circle up

157
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
in some kind of formation

158
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,000
And we're going to make sure we do that by patrols.

159
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,000
And there needs to be

160
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
kind of formal opening,

161
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:11,000
A flag ceremony

162
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:13,000
saying the Scott oath and law, things like that.

163
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:15,000
Some people change that from week to week.

164
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
We have a pretty standard

165
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
approach to that, But

166
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
anything that says: okay,

167
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
here's a tiny, here's a little bit

168
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:25,000
of ceremony, This is serious what we're doing.

169
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Here's the ideals.

170
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
It's going to set the tone for

171
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,000
what happens that evening.

172
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Now, after that opening, we're going to move on to

173
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
skills instruction. Skills instruction

174
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
is a pretty tough nut to crack

175
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
for a lot of people.

176
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
The best thing to do

177
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
is to relate whatever

178
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
is happening in the meeting to the upcoming

179
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:47,000
outing. So try and

180
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
work that skills instruction there.

181
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
somehow We'll do that.

182
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Sometimes we'll do

183
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
an introduction to a merit badge-

184
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Not a merit badge, because

185
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
I don't, because merit badges

186
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
really don't belong at troop

187
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
meetings. You know, I think

188
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
a lot of people do that. I think that's a

189
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
mistaken approach. Don't

190
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
put merit badges in troop meetings. Don't

191
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
do merit badges instruction at troop meetings.

192
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
You're kind of shortcut part of the merit

193
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
badge process. when you do that. You can

194
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:17,000
do the introduction to one, Have a counselor

195
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
there, Have them talk about it, Have them try out

196
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
some skills. It has to be hands on,

197
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
It has to be engaging, It has to be interesting.

198
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
But whatever this skill instruction is,

199
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
it shouldn't be a bunch of scouts sitting

200
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
in chairs listening to somebody talk

201
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
And it certainly should be scouts

202
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
instructing scouts whenever possible.

203
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
So after the skill instruction

204
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
you're going to move on to patrol meetings And at

205
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:39,000
patrol meetings the patrols have some things to do.

206
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:41,000
They need to take attendance,

207
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,000
collect dues, They need to

208
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
plan the patrol's involvement in the

209
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
upcoming troop activities.

210
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
They probably need to select a menu for

211
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
a hike or a camp out.

212
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
They need to assign patrol members to

213
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
specific tasks And they need to

214
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
work out any other

215
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
things that the patrol

216
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
needs to work out. at that point

217
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
The length of the patrol meeting

218
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
really depends on how much business the patrols

219
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,000
have to handle. It might be 5 minutes, It might

220
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
be 20 minutes And that's up to

221
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
the senior patrol leader, as he plans the meeting out,

222
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
to have that ready to go.

223
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
It's also good for the patrol

224
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:17,000
leaders to have a framework

225
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
for their patrol meetings. You know

226
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
they'll be a part of it that's business,

227
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
a part of it that's discussion, a part of it

228
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
that's planning. But they also need

229
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
a good framework to work with And they

230
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
need to keep the pace up and they need to keep it

231
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
moving and engaged. They need to avoid distractions

232
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and they need to get their patrol get

233
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,000
their work done and get ready to go,

234
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Following the patrol meeting and inter-patrol

235
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
activity that you know maybe 15

236
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
to 20 minutes. it can be a game,

237
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
It can be based on the skills instruction

238
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,000
that you've already had. You know, guys, when we

239
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:47,000
come back from patrol meetings we're going to have

240
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
a not-tying relay or something like that.

241
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
So you might want to take some of your

242
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
time at patrol meeting

243
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
to practice some of those skills.

244
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
Inter-patrol competitions should definitely have

245
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,000
some kind of programmatic tie-in

246
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
so that where it relates

247
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
to the other things that the boys are doing

248
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
and the adding that they're preparing to go on,

249
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
And then the meeting

250
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
closes And that is where

251
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,000
the scout master gets to say

252
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
something. At this point,

253
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
when we get to this point in the meeting, the scout

254
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
master should have been observing,

255
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Should have been, you know, towards

256
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
the back of the room watching

257
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
and seeing what's going on. Let the senior

258
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:25,000
patrol leader go through the leadership

259
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
things. Let him make a couple

260
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
of mistakes, help him out if he wants

261
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:31,000
your assistance.

262
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Don't barge in and ask him if he wants your

263
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
assistance. Let him know that you're there,

264
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
that you'll help him out, And you know

265
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,000
you have time to do a scout master's

266
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
minute. And then- and I think it's

267
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
always a good idea in closing

268
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
the meeting to have some kind of a formal

269
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
way of closing- We close

270
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
with the scout master's benediction. May the great master

271
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
of all scouts be with us to a meeting again.

